 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Quaternion Reconstruction:** If numerical error causes $1 - \sum_{i=0}^{2} r_i^2 < 0$, `std::max(0.0f, ...)` ensures the sqrt argument is non-negative [S1, unofficial].
-*   **WASM Memory View:** In JavaScript/WebAssembly implementations, the callback must consume the chunk immediately because the typed memory view detaches if any subsequent WASM allocation grows the heap [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Version Compatibility:** Decoders must check `version` to determine if ZSTD is used (>=4) and which quaternion encoding is active (>=3 uses SmallestThree) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Quaternion Reconstruction:** If numerical error causes $1 - \sum_{i=0}^{2} r_i^2 < 0$, use `std::max(0.0f, ...)` to ensure the sqrt argument is non-negative [S1, unofficial].
+*   **WASM Memory View:** In JavaScript/WebAssembly environments, the typed memory view must be consumed immediately because it detaches if any subsequent WASM allocation grows the heap [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Coordinate Systems:** Extensions like `CoordinateSystemAdobe` may require flipping quaternion components or axes. Check `hadSkippedExtensions` and parse extensions if present [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### SPZ Packed Data Layout
-A variant configuration focusing on the packing options for SH coefficients [S1, unofficial]. It uses `PackOptions::sh1Bits` (defaulting to `DEFAULT_SH1_BITS`=5) and `PackOptions::shRestBits` (defaulting to `DEFAULT_SH_REST_BITS`=4) to specify quantization bit depths for SH degree 1 and degree 2+ coefficients respectively [S1, unofficial]. This reduces entropy for g-zipping during the packing phase [S1, unofficial].
+### [SPZ v4 Format Parsing](7954-spz-v4-format-parsing.md)
+SPZ v4 format parsing is the method for decoding binary SPZ v4 files containing compressed 3D Gaussian splat data into a usable `SplatCloud` structure. It solves the problem of loading high-fidelity Gaussian splat captures in environments lacking native system-level decompression libraries, such as web browsers. It is distinguished from earlier SPZ versions (1–3) by using ZSTD compression per attribute instead of a single gzip stream, and from generic PLY parsers by its specific handling of quantized fields and coordinate system transformations.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Decompression is linear in the number of bytes, dominated by ZSTD decompression and per-Gaussian decoding [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear in the number of Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** ZSTD compression provides significant size reduction, especially for correlated Gaussian data [S1, unofficial]. SmallestThree quaternion encoding offers better precision for small components compared to FirstThree, at the cost of slightly more complex decoding logic [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Low precision storage, efficient compression, support for high-degree SH, flexible extension system [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires custom decoder; non-interleaved layout may be less convenient for some rendering pipelines that expect interleaved data; WASM memory management requires careful handling [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Decompression is linear with respect to the number of bytes, $O(N)$, where $N$ is the file size. Decoding is also $O(N)$.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the decompressed cloud.
+*   **Performance:** ZSTD compression provides high compression ratios for quantized data, reducing file size significantly compared to uncompressed PLY.
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient storage, fast decompression, browser-friendly (via WASM ZSTD), supports high-degree SH.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires ZSTD library for decompression. Non-interleaved layout may complicate certain GPU upload strategies compared to interleaved formats.
+*   **When to Use:** When storage efficiency and fast loading are critical, especially in web or mobile environments.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When random access to individual Gaussian attributes is required without decompressing the entire file, or when ZSTD is not available.
 
 ## Background
-SPZ Packed Format was developed by Niantic Labs as part of their open-source SPZ library for efficient storage and transmission of 3D Gaussian Splatting data [S1, unofficial]. It evolved to support higher precision requirements (SmallestThree quaternions) and compression (ZSTD) in later versions [S1, unofficial]. The format is used in Niantic's AR/VR applications and open-source 3D Gaussian Splatting toolchains [S1, unofficial].
+The SPZ format was developed by Niantic Labs [S1, unofficial]. It is an evolution of earlier Gaussian Splatting storage formats, designed to address the limitations of PLY files in terms of size and loading speed. Version 4 introduced ZSTD compression and SmallestThree quaternion encoding [S1, unofficial].
 
